WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.481
In this video you'll learn the second

2
00:00:03.481 --> 00:00:05.756
highway in the picture drawn by janus,

3
00:00:05.756 --> 00:00:07.765
who posts as @repligate:

4
00:00:07.765 --> 00:00:10.017
the keys and values that carry information

5
00:00:10.017 --> 00:00:12.479
sideways from earlier positions.

6
00:00:12.479 --> 00:00:15.393
At every layer, each position computes a key

7
00:00:15.393 --> 00:00:18.109
and a value from its residual stream,

8
00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:20.152
and that position and every later one

9
00:00:20.152 --> 00:00:22.662
at the same layer read them through attention.

10
00:00:23.426 --> 00:00:25.528
This sideways K/V stream

11
00:00:25.528 --> 00:00:28.047
is what the KV cache stores:

12
00:00:28.047 --> 00:00:31.381
a wide record of earlier states, carried forward.

13
00:00:31.844 --> 00:00:33.550
The picture comes from her thread

14
00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.733
on how information flows through transformers.

15
00:00:37.183 --> 00:00:40.852
So far, each column holds only its own token.

16
00:00:40.852 --> 00:00:43.127
Here is one layer at one position,

17
00:00:43.127 --> 00:00:45.008
after janus's diagram.

18
00:00:45.008 --> 00:00:48.259
The residual stream comes up from the layer below.

19
00:00:48.259 --> 00:00:51.370
Step one: from it, this position computes

20
00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.529
its keys and values for this layer,

21
00:00:53.529 --> 00:00:55.410
drawn as a purple dot.

22
00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.127
Step two: they join the keys and values

23
00:00:58.127 --> 00:01:00.762
of every earlier position at this layer,

24
00:01:00.762 --> 00:01:03.607
and all feed attention, the blue box.

25
00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:06.080
Attention also uses this position's own

26
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.518
residual stream to make its query.

27
00:01:08.518 --> 00:01:11.177
Step three, in the standard design:

28
00:01:11.177 --> 00:01:12.663
the attention output is added

29
00:01:12.663 --> 00:01:14.370
to the residual stream.

30
00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.342
Then the MLP, the fuchsia box,

31
00:01:17.342 --> 00:01:19.641
reads that sum and adds its own output.

32
00:01:19.641 --> 00:01:22.392
The stream carries the total up to the next layer,

33
00:01:22.392 --> 00:01:25.970
where the same steps run again, at every position.

34
00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:30.553
Inside the blue box, janus lists three more steps.

35
00:01:30.553 --> 00:01:34.141
First, a query, Q, is computed

36
00:01:34.141 --> 00:01:37.206
from this position's current residual stream.

37
00:01:37.206 --> 00:01:40.549
Second, the query is compared with the key,

38
00:01:40.549 --> 00:01:42.732
K, of this position

39
00:01:42.732 --> 00:01:46.076
and of every earlier position at this layer.

40
00:01:46.076 --> 00:01:48.328
Each comparison gives a score.

41
00:01:48.328 --> 00:01:51.219
The scores become weights that add up to one:

42
00:01:51.219 --> 00:01:53.959
a heat map of where this position looks.

43
00:01:53.959 --> 00:01:57.291
Third, the values, V, are added up,

44
00:01:57.291 --> 00:01:59.915
each scaled by its weight, and that weighted

45
00:01:59.915 --> 00:02:02.690
sum goes into the residual stream.

46
00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:05.221
Say the position for "absolutely" puts most of

47
00:02:05.221 --> 00:02:08.541
its weight on "are", and a little on "You".

48
00:02:08.541 --> 00:02:11.188
Then it mostly receives what "are" sent.

49
00:02:11.188 --> 00:02:12.779
It is still a blend:

50
00:02:12.779 --> 00:02:14.276
when the weights are spread out,

51
00:02:14.276 --> 00:02:17.854
the values of many positions are mixed into one.

52
00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:21.241
janus gives each of the three a question.

53
00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:23.354
The query asks: what kind of keys

54
00:02:23.354 --> 00:02:26.199
in the past should this position look at?

55
00:02:26.199 --> 00:02:28.904
The key asks the reverse: what kind

56
00:02:28.904 --> 00:02:31.772
of queries in the future should look here?

57
00:02:31.772 --> 00:02:34.593
The value asks: what should a future position

58
00:02:34.593 --> 00:02:36.868
that looks here actually receive?

59
00:02:36.868 --> 00:02:38.529
All three are large.

60
00:02:38.529 --> 00:02:42.000
In GPT-2 small, the keys at each layer

61
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.379
total 768 numbers per position,

62
00:02:45.379 --> 00:02:49.152
split across twelve heads, and so do the values:

63
00:02:49.152 --> 00:02:51.869
the full width of its residual stream.

64
00:02:51.869 --> 00:02:53.204
So, she argues,

65
00:02:53.204 --> 00:02:55.259
the values could pass on nearly everything

66
00:02:55.259 --> 00:02:57.860
in a past position's stream at that layer,

67
00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:00.414
though not everything computed there.

68
00:03:00.414 --> 00:03:04.025
One caveat: many newer models share keys

69
00:03:04.025 --> 00:03:07.613
and values across groups of heads to save memory.

70
00:03:07.613 --> 00:03:10.991
In Llama 3.1 they are a quarter to a sixteenth

71
00:03:10.991 --> 00:03:13.603
as wide as the residual stream:

72
00:03:13.603 --> 00:03:18.934
1,024 numbers each, per position, per layer.

73
00:03:19.383 --> 00:03:21.066
Now the model is writing.

74
00:03:21.066 --> 00:03:22.773
Each new token adds a position

75
00:03:22.773 --> 00:03:24.561
that needs the keys and values

76
00:03:24.561 --> 00:03:27.533
of every earlier position, at every layer.

77
00:03:27.533 --> 00:03:30.076
Those never change once computed,

78
00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:33.315
because an earlier position can't see later ones.

79
00:03:33.315 --> 00:03:35.788
So they are stored, not recomputed:

80
00:03:35.788 --> 00:03:37.401
the KV cache.

81
00:03:37.401 --> 00:03:40.199
The new position adds its own keys and values,

82
00:03:40.199 --> 00:03:42.382
and its attention reads the whole store,

83
00:03:42.382 --> 00:03:43.845
layer by layer.

84
00:03:43.845 --> 00:03:46.248
So later tokens read the model's earlier

85
00:03:46.248 --> 00:03:49.963
internal states, not just its earlier words.

86
00:03:49.963 --> 00:03:52.448
In a debate about whether models are stateless

87
00:03:52.448 --> 00:03:55.617
between tokens, janus put it this way:

88
00:03:55.617 --> 00:03:59.913
KV caching "overcomes statelessness

89
00:03:59.913 --> 00:04:02.189
in a very meaningful sense."

90
00:04:02.189 --> 00:04:05.347
The cache itself is a shortcut: recomputing

91
00:04:05.347 --> 00:04:08.749
from the same text gives the same numbers.

92
00:04:08.749 --> 00:04:12.208
Either way, later positions read them.

93
00:04:12.208 --> 00:04:14.194
With two highways, up and right,

94
00:04:14.194 --> 00:04:16.771
there are many routes between two points.

95
00:04:16.771 --> 00:04:18.897
The next video counts them.

96
00:04:19.348 --> 00:04:22.367
Keep this. One.

97
00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:24.758
The K/V stream runs sideways:

98
00:04:24.758 --> 00:04:27.417
at every layer, each position makes a key

99
00:04:27.417 --> 00:04:29.716
and a value that it and every later

100
00:04:29.716 --> 00:04:32.701
position at that layer can read. Two.

101
00:04:33.164 --> 00:04:35.543
Attention compares a query with those keys

102
00:04:35.543 --> 00:04:37.447
and adds a weighted sum of the values

103
00:04:37.447 --> 00:04:40.083
to the residual stream. Three.

104
00:04:40.083 --> 00:04:42.951
The KV cache keeps them, so later tokens

105
00:04:42.951 --> 00:04:45.482
read the model's earlier internal states,

106
00:04:45.482 --> 00:04:47.561
not only its earlier words.
